      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4 x y + 2 y^{2}&=- 2 x^{2} + 3 y - 18\\\\2 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 3 y + 18&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 3 y + 18$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 3 y + 18)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 3 y + 18)\\,j\\\\&=(4 x + 4 y - 3)\\,i-(4 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 4 x - 4 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-18)&=0\\\\3x-(-18)&=0\\\\3x&=-18\\\\x&=-6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-6,6)$."
    },
