      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2 x y + y^{2}&=- x^{2} + 4 y + 16\\\\x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 4 y - 16&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 4 y - 16$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 4 y - 16)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 4 y - 16)\\,j\\\\&=(2 x + 2 y - 4)\\,i-(2 x + 2 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 2 x - 2 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}1(x+(-x))^{2}-4(-x)-(16)&=0\\\\4x-(16)&=0\\\\4x&=16\\\\x&=4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(4,-4)$."
    },
