      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}6 x y + 3 y^{2}&=- 3 x^{2} + 2 y - 6\\\\3 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 2 y + 6&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 2 y + 6$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 2 y + 6)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 2 y + 6)\\,j\\\\&=(6 x + 6 y - 2)\\,i-(6 x + 6 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 6 x - 6 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}3(x+(-x))^{2}-2(-x)-(-6)&=0\\\\2x-(-6)&=0\\\\2x&=-6\\\\x&=-3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-3,3)$."
